# Eastern Keel | Mobile First Pass | 2026

*Standards verified June 2026. Assumes Tailwind CSS v4 and Next.js 16.2 App Router. A legacy appendix for Tailwind v3 and Next 14 to 15 sits at the bottom.*

## When to run this

Run this after the desktop design is approved and the base site is built. This is a mobile pass, not a redesign. You are adapting an approved design to perform on the smallest screens your audience actually uses, then scaling up. Do not change the design intent. Adapt layout, spacing, and hierarchy.

One structural change for 2026 makes this pass much smaller than it used to be. If components were built container query first during the desktop build (see the design language document), most of them already adapt to narrow space on their own. This pass then handles the page frame, the navigation, touch ergonomics, and the few components that still need viewport level decisions.

---

## The baseline devices

**Design floor: 360px CSS viewport width.**

The old "375px iPhone SE floor" is out of date. The SE was discontinued in February 2025 and no current iPhone is narrower than 390px. More to the point, Android's 360px workhorse width is the single most common narrow viewport worldwide, more common than any one iPhone width. If it works at 360px, it works everywhere.

If a project's analytics are heavily US and iOS skewed, 375px is an acceptable floor, since a large installed base of SE and 12 and 13 mini devices still sit at 375px. When in doubt, build to 360px.

| Device | CSS viewport width |
|---|---|
| Common Android (Galaxy A series and similar) | 360px |
| iPhone SE 2 and 3, 12 and 13 mini (legacy, still in use) | 375px |
| iPhone 17e, iPhone 12 to 16 standard | 390px |
| iPhone 17 and 17 Pro | 402px |
| Pixel and Galaxy flagships, iPhone 11 and XR class | 412px to 414px |
| iPhone Air | 420px |
| iPhone 16 and 17 Pro Max | 440px |

**Target 360px. Test at 360px, 375px, 390px, 402px, and 440px before shipping.**

---

## Container queries: the primitive that changes this pass

A component should respond to the width of the space it sits in, not the width of the browser window. A card in a wide hero and the same card in a narrow related list are the same component in different containers, and each should adapt itself. This removes most of the viewport breakpoint guesswork that mobile passes used to be made of.

Mark the wrapper as a container, then query it:

```css
.card-frame { container-type: inline-size; container-name: card; }

/* base: stacked, the narrow default */
.card { display: grid; gap: var(--spacing-sm); }

/* the card itself decides when it has room to go horizontal */
@container card (min-width: 30rem) {
  .card { grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr; }
}
```

In Tailwind v4, the same logic is available through the container query variants. Mark the container with `@container` and prefix utilities with `@`:

```html
<div class="@container">
  <article class="grid gap-4 @md:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"> ... </article>
</div>
```

The rule of thumb for this pass: anything reusable responds to its container, the page frame and the major section splits respond to the viewport.

---

## Viewport breakpoints for the page frame

Tailwind is mobile first. Unprefixed utilities apply at all sizes. Prefixed utilities apply at that breakpoint and above. Use these only for the page frame and the big section splits, not for component internals.

- no prefix: mobile, 360px and up. This is the base.
- `sm:` 640px and up
- `md:` 768px and up
- `lg:` 1024px and up
- `xl:` 1280px and up
- `2xl:` 1536px and up

Tailwind v4 configures breakpoints in CSS through the `@theme` block with `--breakpoint-*` variables, not in a config file. Adjust them there if a project needs custom stops.

